<h2>Why 48 hours is the line that matters</h2>
<p>Anticipatory bail under Section 482 of the BNSS (formerly Section 438 CrPC) protects a person who apprehends arrest on an accusation of a non-bailable offence. For a government servant, the stakes of that protection are defined by service law rather than criminal law. Rule 10(2) of the CCS (Classification, Control and Appeal) Rules, 1965 provides that a government servant who is detained in custody — whether on a criminal charge or otherwise — for a period exceeding forty-eight hours shall be deemed to have been placed under suspension. No disciplinary authority needs to apply its mind; the suspension follows from the fact of detention itself.</p>
<p>Deemed suspension is not a minor administrative note. It typically brings reduced subsistence allowance in place of full pay, withdrawal from duties, a pause on promotions and empanelment, and an entry that shadows the officer through vigilance clearances for years. Similar deemed-suspension provisions exist in state civil services rules and in the conduct, discipline and appeal rules of most public sector undertakings and banks, so the forty-eight-hour logic extends well beyond central government employment. This is why, for a serving officer, the anticipatory bail application is frequently the single most consequential filing in the entire case: it is the difference between contesting an allegation as a serving officer and contesting it as a suspended one.</p>

<h2>Two proceedings, two standards</h2>
<p>An FIR against a government servant usually sets two machines in motion at once, and they do not wait for each other.</p>
<div class="compare">
  <div class="col old"><h4>Criminal case</h4><p>Investigated by police or a specialised agency, tried before a criminal court, and decided on proof beyond reasonable doubt. Arrest, bail and trial are governed by the BNSS.</p></div>
  <div class="arrow">→</div>
  <div class="col new"><h4>Departmental proceedings</h4><p>Initiated by the disciplinary authority under the service rules, inquired into departmentally, and decided on the preponderance of probabilities. The outcome affects pay, rank and continuance in service.</p></div>
</div>
<p>The two proceedings are independent and can run in parallel; neither the pendency of one nor, ordinarily, its outcome automatically concludes the other. An officer planning the defence must therefore plan both tracks together: a statement made or document produced in one forum will be read in the other, and the sequencing of the two defences deserves deliberate thought rather than improvisation.</p>

<h2>The anticipatory bail application: what it should do</h2>
<p>An application under Section 482 BNSS for a serving officer follows the ordinary structure — the accusation, the apprehension of arrest, and the reasons custodial interrogation is unnecessary — but several elements deserve particular care.</p>
<div class="check">
  <ul>
    <li>Full and candid disclosure of the official position held, the department, and any connection between the allegation and official duties.</li>
    <li>A precise account of cooperation already extended: notices received, appearances made, documents supplied.</li>
    <li>The documentary nature of the allegation, where applicable, and the fact that the relevant records are already with the department or the investigating agency.</li>
    <li>Roots in the community and in service: a serving officer with a fixed posting, a government residence and a traceable salary is by definition not a flight risk in the ordinary sense.</li>
    <li>Disclosure of any departmental proceedings already initiated on the same facts.</li>
  </ul>
</div>
<p>Candour about the official position cuts both ways and must be handled honestly. The position explains why the officer is traceable and rooted; it also explains why the prosecution may argue influence over subordinates and records. An application that addresses that concern head-on — by volunteering conditions — is stronger than one that hopes the point will not be raised.</p>

<h2>Conditions commonly imposed</h2>
<p>Where anticipatory bail is granted to a serving officer, the conditions tend to follow a recognisable pattern: joining the investigation as and when called and cooperating fully; not influencing witnesses, and in particular not contacting subordinates connected with the allegation or the records; not tampering with official files; not leaving the country without the leave of the court, often with surrender of the passport; and, in cases connected with the current charge of the officer, courts sometimes note any transfer or change of desk that the department has effected. Conditions of this kind are directed at protecting the investigation, and compliance with them should be documented, because the record of compliance matters if the state later seeks cancellation.</p>

<h2>Dealing with the department</h2>
<p>Service jurisprudence places a duty of candour on the government servant. Conduct rules across services require intimation of criminal proceedings, and experience suggests that suppression is treated more harshly than the underlying allegation. The sensible course is usually prompt written intimation to the controlling authority of the FIR and of the grant of anticipatory bail, through proper channel, keeping the language factual and brief. The order granting protection is itself a useful document in the departmental file: it records that a court considered custodial interrogation unnecessary, which, while not binding on the disciplinary authority, frames the officer as a cooperating litigant rather than an absconder.</p>

<h2>Conviction, acquittal and the service record</h2>
<p>The endgame also differs for officers. A conviction can constitute an independent ground for departmental penalty under the service rules, up to dismissal in serious cases, quite apart from the sentence. An acquittal does not automatically entitle the officer to have the suspension period treated as duty or to recover full pay for it; those questions are decided under the service rules, with the nature of the acquittal and the conduct of the officer weighing in the decision. What the officer can control at the outset is the forty-eight-hour question — and that is precisely what a well-prepared anticipatory bail application is for.</p>

<div class="note"><p>Nothing in this explainer is specific to any investigating agency or category of offence. Where the allegation arises under a special statute with its own bail restrictions, the anticipatory bail analysis changes and must be assessed on the statute actually invoked.</p></div>
